  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a ridge is to the west over the Great Plains. A \ntrough is over the Northeast. The Tennesee Valley is between these \ntwo features. A minor wave is likely to move through the region this \nafternoon bringing isolated and possibly scattered showers and \nstorms east of I-75, especially close to the mountains and NE \nTennessee/SW Virginia. The North Carolina counties are on the fringe \nof the slight SPC risk for today, meanwhile, a marginal risk\nextends into southeast Tennessee and along the mountains. Thus,\nstrong to severe thunderstorms possible this afternoon and evening.\nThe primary threat will be gusty winds.\n\nAt the surface, a cold front will move through the region in the \nmorning hours. Slightly cooler temps expected today with highs \nmainly in the upper 80s and lower 90s. Although, not so much near \nChattanooga, perhaps the cold front will not move through the \nsouthern Valley until the afternoon hours. It is moving very slow at \nthe moment and currently near the Ohio River. \n\nIn the upper levels, the ridge builds back into the region by Monday \nafternoon although centered near Oklahoma. Monday will have cooler \ntemps (although still very warm) with highs in the Tennessee Valley \nmainly in the upper 80s. Showers and storms will be isolated to \nscattered Monday afternoon as an inverted surface trough forms over \nKentucky and Tennessee. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday will be dry as the ridge weakens but is now \ncentered closer to the region over the Lower Mississippi Valley. \nHighs will be in the upper 80s and lower 90s which is a few degrees \nabove normal.\n\nThursday and Friday a broad trough moves into the Tennessee Valley \nalong with a surface front. Scattered showers and storms are likely \nif this pattern holds.",
